    "title": "AP News Report",
    "text": "• Cities around the world welcomed 2026 with fireworks and light shows.\n• In New York City, revelers celebrated in Times Square despite cold temperatures.\n• Rio de Janeiro held concerts and a fireworks show on Copacabana Beach.\n• Hong Kong held limited celebrations following a recent fire.\n• Australia held celebrations less than a month after a mass shooting.\n• Jack Smith stated that the January 6, 2021, riot at the U.S. Capitol “does not happen” without Donald Trump.\n• Smith said Trump was the “most culpable and most responsible person” in the criminal conspiracy to overturn the 2020 election.\n• Chief Justice John Roberts stated that the Constitution remains a sturdy pillar for the country.\n• National Guard members arrived in New Orleans for New Year's celebrations.\n• The National Guard is assisting with safety measures nearly a year after a truck attack on Bourbon Street.\n• Zohran Mamdani will become mayor of New York City in 2026.\n• Mamdani will have two swearing-in ceremonies: a private one in a subway station and a public one outside City Hall.\n• Donald Trump vetoed two bipartisan bills.\n• One bill was for drinking water pipeline legislation and the other gave the Miccosukee Tribe of Indians of Florida more control of some of its tribal lands.\n• A deep-sea search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 began in the Indian Ocean.\n• China's People's Liberation Army said it “successfully completed” military exercises in waters off Taiwan.\n• Trains are running again through the Channel Tunnel after a power malfunction.\n• Thailand released 18 Cambodian prisoners of war as part of a ceasefire agreement.",
